    title: "SpaceX wins U.S. government approval to launch space data centers",
    summary: "SpaceX's plans to launch as many as 1 million satellites have raised concerns from scientists and others in the space industry.",
    preview: "The U.S. Federal Communications Commission (FCC) has approved SpaceX’s request to launch as many as 1 million satellites for the creation of a network of data centers in orbit for complex artificial intelligence computing. The agency’s first-ever authorization of data centers in space makes way for a concept that was central to Elon Musk’s pitch to investors ahead of SpaceX’s initial public offering in June. The company has cautioned that orbital AI data centers rely on unproven technologies and may never be commercially viable.",
    body: "The U.S. Federal Communications Commission (FCC) has approved SpaceX’s request to launch as many as 1 million satellites for the creation of a network of data centers in orbit for complex artificial intelligence computing.\n\nThe agency’s first-ever authorization of data centers in space makes way for a concept that was central to Elon Musk’s pitch to investors ahead of SpaceX’s initial public offering in June. The company has cautioned that orbital AI data centers rely on unproven technologies and may never be commercially viable.\n\n“By moving compute capacity beyond Earth, SpaceX and the many competitors working on their own orbital data centers are meeting the moment,” FCC Chairman Brendan Carr said Wednesday in a statement. He has frequently praised SpaceX and attended a test launch of the company’s new Starship rocket earlier this month.",
